#heading(level:1)[#text("Verdict on every original claim")]

#text("These verdicts concern the supplied claims and the fresh implementations in this PR. The historical producer, seeds, pricing definitions and raw 1,260 runs remain unlocated. Fresh agreement does not authenticate an exact historical statistic. All cost intervals below are ratios of sums over matched targets at explicitly declared prices, not confidence intervals. Scope is the tested synthetic families and native backends.")


#heading(level:2)[#text("1. Subfield decomposition")]

#table(columns:(1.1fr,.8fr,3.3fr),inset:4pt,stroke:.3pt+rgb("cccccc"),table.header([#text("Original claim")],[#text("Verdict")],[#text("Fresh evidence and qualification")]),[#text("Odd characteristic n=2 through 5 matches a random base of the same size.")],[#text("Broadly compatible in small tested cases; equivalence not established.")],[#text("24 primary curves: p=5,7; n=2,3,4,5; three curves/cell; 32 actual-point-count matched controls/curve. Maximum nominal absolute z=2.087. Seven of eight cell intervals lie within a loose ±0.05 coverage margin; the remaining p=5,n=2 interval extends to about +0.095. Random-x and matched-point nulls are separately retained.")],[#text("Weil descent creates no extra relations there.")],[#text("Not proved by coverage.")],[#text("A finite coverage census tests reachable targets, not relation multiplicities or every solver. Eight descended-coefficient odd controls demonstrate that model selection matters. No universal theorem follows.")],[#text("Binary n=2 is the exception.")],[#text("Too narrow as a theoretical statement.")],[#text("Trace from an even-degree extension annihilates every subfield element. The same character obstruction applies to fixed even summand counts in other even extension degrees under the normalized ordinary binary model. Only n=2 is costed end to end here.")],[#text("Binary coverage tends exactly to 3/8.")],[#text("Finite trend reproduced; universal limit unproved.")],[#text("Mean full-group coverage at q=512 is 0.3731522954 on three primary non-descended curves; opposite-coset controls give 0.3765374531. Descended-b controls approach 1/2 or give only the identity (1/N), so the trace restriction alone cannot imply 3/8. Seven q levels, three primary curves each.")],[#text("Expected random coverage is 0.3935.")],[#text("Occupancy approximation, not an exact finite null.")],[#text("1−exp(−1/2)=0.3934693403 is the usual heuristic at this density. Actual matched random-base mean at q=512 is 0.3920743848. Full finite counts and 32 controls/curve replace the approximation in comparisons.")],[#text("z=−4.7.")],[#text("Exact historical statistic unverified; not reproduced.")],[#text("Fresh q=512 paired-curve nominal z=−160.3900 under the declared control construction. Only three deterministic primary curves at that level; this is not the original statistic or a calibrated universal significance statement.")],[#text("Every x in F_q has trace zero over F_(q²).")],[#text("Verified, with trace convention explicit.")],[#text("Absolute trace to F_2 is zero. Relative trace to F_q is also zero in characteristic two. The group character is absolute Tr(x+a), not simply an unqualified trace of x.")],[#text("The whole factor base sits in one coset of 2E; checked on 21 curves.")],[#text("Verified for the ordinary normalized binary model.")],[#text("On all 21 primary curves, exhaustive doubled images equal the character kernel and every rational lift of subfield x has one fixed character. Two summands therefore land in 2E, whatever that coset is. See the retained trace checks and complete census.")],[#text("Only half the group can ever decompose.")],[#text("Correct upper bound for the full group, not exact coverage.")],[#text("Two-summand targets belong to the index-two subgroup 2E. The odd-prime DLP subgroup already lies inside 2E, so half of the DLP targets are not ruled out.")],[#text("About 4.7% fewer relations per target.")],[#text("Misstated unit.")],[#text("(0.3934693403−0.375)/0.3934693403 is about 4.69%, a comparison of whole-group hit probabilities. It is not a measured reduction in relation multiplicity or subgroup DLP yield. Fresh measured whole-group coverage is retained without that substitution.")],[#text("A free trace test skips impossible targets.")],[#text("Useful filter; setup is not free.")],[#text("168 additional end-to-end solves retain identical stopping positions. The test skips 3,044/6,046 full-group relation candidates; none of the odd-order DLP targets fails it. A cold n(n−1)-squaring mask and logical per-candidate work are charged. At q=512 arithmetic cost is 0.971–0.987 of the unfiltered route; the native sensitivity reaches 1.004.")],[#text("End-to-end n=2 costs 2.7–66× rho in every pricing cell.")],[#text("Arithmetic loss reproduced; numerical range replaced.")],[#text("168 binary and 40 eligible odd-quadratic targets, all verified. Per-curve binary pipeline/negation-rho ratios span 2.089–70.311 over the 12 arithmetic prices (up to 113.842 with native sensitivity). Odd quadratic spans 2.079–3.505. Original prices/reference were unavailable. Every tested per-curve arithmetic cell loses to rho. Tiny binary q=8,16 and odd p=5,7 pipelines use fewer instrumented retired instructions than rho-neg (0.4491, 0.4942, 0.4363, 0.4854 respectively); that is a separate unit, not a qualified wall-speed claim.")],[#text("Root finding costs about 1,000 multiplications per call.")],[#text("Not an intrinsic average requirement.")],[#text("Complete oracle M/call averages range 12.141–16.187 in binary and 46.270–150.778 in odd quadratic. These include rational-lift checks but list squarings/inversions separately. They are not full M-equivalent prices or worst-case root bounds. Binary linearized Artin–Schreier solving avoids a generic degree-polynomial root routine.")])

#heading(level:2)[#text("2. Prime fields and PKM tower bases")]

#table(columns:(1.1fr,.8fr,3.3fr),inset:4pt,stroke:.3pt+rgb("cccccc"),table.header([#text("Original claim")],[#text("Verdict")],[#text("Fresh evidence and qualification")]),[#text("Kummer and Dickson: 18 cells, at least 23 primes each.")],[#text("Fresh design executed.")],[#text("Two families × depths 2,3,4 × three prime bands; exactly 23 eligible primes/cell, 414 fixtures. Rejections, construction specs and exact fiber bases retained. This does not recover the historical 18 cells.")],[#text("Coverage indistinguishable from random x sets; absolute z≤1.84 everywhere.")],[#text("Bound not reproduced; effects are small.")],[#text("Maximum absolute nominal z=4.2548 under controls matched by actual point count, maximum absolute coverage difference 0.0000898746. Supplementary unconditional uniform x sets give maximum absolute z=2.8665. Nulls differ; the latter was added after the conditional analysis. Neither bound is 1.84; no unadjusted cell statistic establishes a universal effect.")],[#text("Tower structure creates no extra relations.")],[#text("Universal statement unsupported.")],[#text("Coverage differences do not count all relations or prove random equivalence. Complete fixed-fiber and random-base sumsets are available. Small departures occur; no useful end-to-end advantage appears for this implemented two-summand route.")],[#text("Any PKM advantage must come from solving, which was not measured.")],[#text("Fresh solving measured; other solvers remain open.")],[#text("3,312 targets, both structured and matched-random pipelines plus plain/negation rho; 13,248 solves, all verified. Structured pipeline arithmetic ratios span 4.263–1695.104 across per-curve cells versus negation rho. Of 6,624 pipeline returns, 562 precede any decomposition, including 64 empty-base returns: generic identity hits, not pure IC evidence. This tests fixed Kummer/Dickson fibers, not every auxiliary-elliptic-isogeny or multivariate solver from PKM.")])

#heading(level:2)[#text("3. Genus-2 transfer and index calculus")]

#table(columns:(1.1fr,.8fr,3.3fr),inset:4pt,stroke:.3pt+rgb("cccccc"),table.header([#text("Original claim")],[#text("Verdict")],[#text("Fresh evidence and qualification")]),[#text("C/F_p has Jacobian isogenous to the Weil restriction of E/F_(p²), with a checked transfer tau.")],[#text("Implemented for an explicit published family.")],[#text("Scholten/Bernstein–Lange construction; one-infinity quintic 12Y²=12+100t+299t²+380t³+187t⁴+30t⁵. Pullback plus conjugation and the dual norm satisfy norm(tau(P))=2P. Exact integer polynomial cover certificate, exhaustive small-field map/norm checks and independent zeta/order counts on eight fields; 32 seeded map/additivity checks on each of 42 primary models. The geometric theorem is sourced, not newly formally proved.")],[#text("1,260 runs through 40 bits, each checked by k·P.")],[#text("Fresh requirement exceeded, with size ambiguity resolved.")],[#text("Two separate 1,260-target cohorts (field-indexed and exact prime-subgroup-indexed), four arms each: 10,080 primary genus solves. All pass source-curve verification. Actual subgroup sizes are exactly 16,20,24,28,32,36,40 bits in the second cohort; its largest fields are 44 bits. The first cohort's nominal field labels must not be read as subgroup bits.")],[#text("IC solves through the first large-prime cycle.")],[#text("False for the implemented signed graph.")],[#text("In the actual-order cohort, 1,244/1,260 solves require a later useful dependence; only 16 solve at the first graph cycle. Negative cycles can anchor a large-prime variable without identifying k. At actual 40 bits, 179/180 solve later.")],[#text("IC uses 0.27–0.37·p Jacobian operations.")],[#text("Search-count trend reproduced; cold count differs at small sizes.")],[#text("Actual-order mean walk trials/p range 0.27479–0.31520. Cold Jacobian operations/p range 0.92912 at 16 bits down to 0.31268 at 40 bits. Setup, scalar initialization, transfer and verification cannot be dropped when calling this end to end.")],[#text("Rho needs 0.31–0.44·p operations.")],[#text("Mostly consistent with plain-rho search at larger tested sizes; reference matters.")],[#text("Plain-rho walk trials/p are 0.32092–0.33669 from 20 through 40 bits; 16-bit value is 0.25287. Actual 40-bit cold source operations/p=0.32406. Stronger negation-rho search is 0.23709·p there. Counts scale with sqrt(r), so p-normalization also depends on the cofactor.")],[#text("Independent random trials give the same count.")],[#text("Compatible counts, not demonstrated equality.")],[#text("21 independent controls per cohort: order normalized trial ratio 1.04439 (curve-bootstrap 95% 0.89882–1.20455); field ratio 0.89860 [0.73878,1.09754]. Order-cohort independent trials cost 25.766× primary at default prices, including fresh scalar generation. Another 21 unfiltered controls per cohort are separate.")],[#text("Therefore this is not rho hiding in the walk.")],[#text("Mechanism audited; statistical argument alone insufficient.")],[#text("Primary extraction uses signed relation dependencies; repeated walk states trigger charged restarts rather than rho extraction, and repeated split divisor classes are discarded up to sign. Independent sampling is a separate charged control. Equal-looking stopping counts alone would not establish the mechanism.")],[#text("A Jacobian addition costs 41M+I.")],[#text("Not this backend.")],[#text("100 generic full-degree additions on the largest fixture each cost 70M+3S+I in base-F_p units. Cantor exceptional cases use a checked fallback. This is an implementation measurement, not a lower bound on optimized Jacobian arithmetic.")],[#text("E(F_(p²)) addition costs 12M+I.")],[#text("Compatible at a stated squaring price.")],[#text("Generic source additions cost 9M+4S+I in base-F_p units. At S/M=0.75 this is exactly 12M+I; other price cells differ.")],[#text("Ranking unresolved at every size; cost ratio 0.51–2.83.")],[#text("Not reproduced; resolved within the declared fresh model.")],[#text("All 14 cohort/size means lose to negation rho in all 12 arithmetic cells. Actual 40-bit subgroup IC/rho-neg=1.42081–18.36249; IC/plain-rho=1.07626–13.93230. Native sensitivity preserves the actual-40-bit range. This is a finite implementation/model verdict, not a proof that every genus-2 algorithm loses.")],[#text("At 40 bits rho wins 10 of 12 cells.")],[#text("Replaced by 12/12.")],[#text("Both source references win all 12 arithmetic cells at actual 40-bit subgroup size; negation rho also wins all 12 at nominal field-40. Ratios use complete cold work.")],[#text("Measured gap about 2.8×.")],[#text("Not reproduced in these observations.")],[#text("Actual-order-40 default arithmetic gap=11.09091× versus negation rho, 8.40978× versus plain rho. Complete instrumented retired-instruction gaps=7.48684× [6.65709,8.37164] and 5.78231× [5.21506,6.42857], respectively. Five-pair rho A/A range 0.9999703–1.0000121. L2 timing preflights rejected host contention; no qualified wall-time gap is asserted.")])

#text("The original operation formulas also impose a consistency check. If both quoted operation-count ranges are complete and use a common nonnegative I/M price, their simplified IC/rho ratio is at least (0.27/0.44)·(41+I)/(12+I), hence at least 27/44 (about 0.614). A value of 0.51 therefore needs different populations, extra reference costs or a different accounting convention; it cannot be derived from those four supplied ranges alone.")


